 his voice  he gave it to me and you know if you  picked up the phone years ago you really  people couldn't tell which one of us was  answering the phone I think we're pretty  close to being sounding identical and I  know you know this and but your kids  have this archival footage of dad I mean  there that's always your voice will  always be there never thought of it that  way I until you just said it that way  you know all those all those clips you  know they'll fill out they'll outlive me  because I've been again given the great  gift of being at some of these  significant events just dumb luck I  happen to be there when Tiger won the  night  1897 masters and he he closed it out  with a four-foot putt at the 18th to to  win by a dozen shots and and and I and I  said there it is a win for the ages well  that little narrative over that who will  be played back a hundred years from now  when somebody builds the opening montage  and tees for the Masters that's a moment  that's that's it's evergreen  transcendent moment that will go on in  time I didn't really think about it at  that time whatever it might be a Super  Bowl finish or a Gordon Hayward nearly  making the shot the half-court heap at  the end of the game those clips are  gonna be played back for a long time  you just hope you do them right but do  you think that you truly captured like  is there a moment you talk about tiger  is that the one that you go got it  like I captured the moment in the way  that it needed to be captured no regrets  you always have a little bit of doubt in  the back of your head because it's live  and you're not sure if you did it right  in you go back and listen to a show and  it's especially if it's like the next  day it never sounds like anything of it  any part of it was was that great not  that it ever is great but you got that  one though the tiger well can I tell you  a brief story I know he probably up  against the clock here but I felt some  pressure of the night before because  that moment was on the way  and Tiger led by 9 going into Sunday and  he was gonna become the youngest  champion he was gonna set the 72-hole  scoring record he was gonna have the  largest margin of victory and then of  course the social significance of Tiger  coming to Augusta and winning I mean  this was a gigantic moment and I gave a  lot of thought and some people said oh  you've you pre-planned that why you  scripted that I know you didn't just  come up with that but you know there's  certain situations where you know what's  gonna happen if I didn't think it  through the night before I wasn't doing  my job for example if I was a writer and  I was covering the 1997 masters and on  Saturday night with Tiger leading by  nine if you weren't thinking of how you  were gonna write your lead the next day  and instead you decide to go out and  have dinner and  you know three or four beers and a  bottle of wine you're not doing your job  somebody should take your press pass  away the people that are paying your  expenses your job is to go down there  and document what's gonna happen I knew  I was sitting on the precipice of  history and I felt figuratively  I felt the presence over my shoulder of  Jim McKay Jack Whittaker Pat Summerall  they were all still alive and I knew  that at that time I knew they would be  watching the next day and I knew they  would want to see how that moment was  handled so I knew it had to be succinct  I didn't know it was gonna be a  three-foot putt but you knew he was  gonna win and I've I just thought of  what could last in time was something as  important as his victory and ultimately  I thought to describe it as a win for  the ages would be the appropriate line  for that moment it was a you captured it  you think it was the right one I didn't  know it's told so you just validated it  I'm serious I mean I've never really  talked about it but I hear it all the  time it plays back on all the promos and  openings and things like that I